add('requests','docs/user/quickstart.rst',[
('In the Requests quickstart, why might one key in a query-parameter dictionary be absent from the final URL?', 'Note that any dictionary key whose value is ``None`` will not be added to the\nURL\'s query string.', 'Query-string entries whose dictionary value is None are omitted.'),
('A server returned a parseable JSON error document. What does the quickstart recommend checking before calling the request successful?', 'It should be noted that the success of the call to ``r.json()`` does **not**\nindicate the success of the response. Some servers may return a JSON object in a\nfailed response (e.g. error details with HTTP 500). Such JSON will be decoded\nand returned. To check that a request is successful, use\n``r.raise_for_status()`` or check ``r.status_code`` is what you expect.', 'Successful JSON decoding does not establish HTTP success; check raise_for_status or the expected status_code.'),
('Which payload argument loses precedence if a caller supplies form data or uploaded files as well?', 'Note, the ``json`` parameter is ignored if either ``data`` or ``files`` is passed.', 'The json parameter is ignored when data or files is supplied.'),
('How is the redirect response history ordered in the introductory Requests guide?', 'The :attr:`Response.history <requests.Response.history>` list contains the\n:class:`Response <requests.Response>` objects that were created in order to\ncomplete the request. The list is sorted from the oldest to the most recent\nresponse.', 'Response.history is ordered from the oldest redirect response to the most recent.'),
('Why is a five-second Requests timeout not necessarily a five-second cap on downloading the complete response?', '``timeout`` is not a time limit on the entire response download;\n    rather, an exception is raised if the server has not issued a\n    response for ``timeout`` seconds', 'The timeout concerns a period without a response/socket data, not a total-download deadline.'),
])
add('requests','docs/user/advanced.rst',[
('I repeatedly call the same host and want both cookies and established connections reused. Which documented Requests object provides that?', 'The Session object allows you to persist certain parameters across\nrequests. It also persists cookies across all requests made from the\nSession instance, and will use ``urllib3``\'s `connection pooling`_.', 'A Session persists cookies and uses connection pooling across its requests.'),
('How should a manually customized prepared request be built so that session cookies are still applied?', ':class:`Session <requests.Session>`-level state such as cookies will\nnot get applied to your request. To get a\n:class:`PreparedRequest <requests.PreparedRequest>` with that state\napplied, replace the call to :meth:`Request.prepare()\n<requests.Request.prepare>` with a call to\n:meth:`Session.prepare_request() <requests.Session.prepare_request>`', 'Use Session.prepare_request rather than Request.prepare to apply session state.'),
('A streamed response was only partly consumed. What must happen before its connection can return to the pool?', 'If you set ``stream`` to ``True`` when making a request, Requests cannot\nrelease the connection back to the pool unless you consume all the data or call\n:meth:`Response.close <requests.Response.close>`.', 'Consume all response data or explicitly close the response before its connection is released.'),
('Where is the usage pattern for setting connection establishment and response-read waiting limits separately?', 'The timeout value will be applied to both the ``connect`` and the ``read``\ntimeouts. Specify a tuple if you would like to set the values separately::\n\n    r = requests.get(\'https://github.com\', timeout=(3.05, 27))', 'A two-element timeout tuple sets connect and read timeouts separately.'),
('Why may connecting to a hostname with several unreachable addresses take longer than the configured connection timeout?', 'The connect timeout applies to each connection attempt to an IP address.\n          If multiple addresses exist for a domain name, the underlying ``urllib3`` will\n          try each address sequentially until one successfully connects.', 'The connect timeout applies to each IP-address attempt and addresses are tried sequentially.'),
])
